        why:'A language model produces a probability distribution over the next token. Decoding policy determines how that distribution becomes text.',
        analogy:'The model is a panel of possible next words with confidence scores. Decoding is the rule used to choose the next card and repeat the process.',
        explanation:'The forward pass produces logits; softmax converts them into a probability distribution. Greedy decoding chooses the highest-probability token. Temperature rescales logits before softmax: lower values sharpen the distribution and higher values flatten it. Top-k limits candidates to the k most likely tokens; top-p keeps the smallest set whose cumulative probability reaches p. Beam search explores multiple candidate sequences and is useful for some constrained tasks but can produce repetitive text. Stop sequences, maximum output tokens and structured decoding constrain completion. Temperature does not make the model more knowledgeable or factually correct.',
        keyPoints:['Logits are unnormalized scores; softmax produces probabilities.','Temperature changes distribution sharpness, not factual grounding.','Top-k and top-p truncate the candidate token set.','Greedy decoding is deterministic for a fixed model and input in typical setups.','Use output limits and stop conditions to control latency and cost.'],
        workedExample:'For a JSON extraction task, use a low-variance decoding policy, a strict schema and server-side validation. For brainstorming, allow a broader sampling distribution but still validate claims before use.',
        code:'import numpy as np\n\ndef softmax(logits, temperature=1.0):\n    scaled = np.asarray(logits, dtype=float) / max(temperature, 1e-6)\n    exp = np.exp(scaled - scaled.max())\n    return exp / exp.sum()\n\nlogits = [2.4, 1.2, 0.2]\nprint(softmax(logits, 0.5))  # sharper\nprint(softmax(logits, 1.5))  # flatter',
        steps:[{title:'Compute scores',detail:'Run the model on the prompt and obtain next-token logits.',input:'Context token IDs',output:'Vocabulary logits',failure:'Context truncation changes the effective prompt.'},{title:'Apply decoding policy',detail:'Apply temperature, top-k or top-p and any constrained decoding rules.',input:'Logits and decoding settings',output:'Candidate distribution',failure:'Invalid settings create empty or degenerate candidate sets.'},{title:'Select token',detail:'Choose a token and append it to the context.',input:'Candidate distribution',output:'Next token',failure:'Sampling is mistaken for semantic verification.'},{title:'Stop safely',detail:'Check stop tokens, output budget and structured response validation.',input:'Generated sequence',output:'Bounded completion',failure:'Unbounded generation or malformed output.'}],
        question:{prompt:'What does increasing temperature usually do?',options:['Makes the model’s knowledge more current','Flattens the next-token distribution and increases sampling diversity','Guarantees factual correctness','Removes the context window'],explanation:'Higher temperature generally makes lower-probability tokens more likely; it changes sampling diversity, not knowledge or truth.'},
        project:'Implement softmax temperature and top-k sampling over a toy vocabulary. Compare deterministic greedy output with sampled output and document how stop and token limits affect behavior.',
