          why:'Long-running agent tasks may pause, fail or resume. Without explicit workflow state, retries can repeat actions and lose important decisions.',
          analogy:'An airport departure board records whether a flight is scheduled, boarding, delayed or departed. The next action depends on the current state, not on rereading every announcement.',
          explanation:'Model workflow state as explicit states and allowed transitions. Persist checkpoints after meaningful steps, including input references, output references, version and idempotency keys. Use retries only for classified transient failures and make every side effect idempotent. Store approval as a durable state transition. A resumed workflow must revalidate authorization and data freshness. Use compensation actions for multi-step business operations when true atomic transactions are unavailable. Keep event history append-only enough to reconstruct what happened without storing unnecessary sensitive payloads.',
          keyPoints:['Use explicit states and allowed transitions.','Checkpoint after verified steps and before long waits.','Make side effects idempotent and classify retryable errors.','Persist human approval as a state transition.','Revalidate authorization and data freshness when resuming.'],
          workedExample:'A refund workflow has requested, policy_checked, awaiting_approval, submitted and completed states. If the process restarts after submission, the idempotency key and payment provider reference prevent a second refund.',
          code:'ALLOWED = {\n  "requested": {"policy_checked", "rejected"},\n  "policy_checked": {"awaiting_approval", "rejected"},\n  "awaiting_approval": {"submitted", "rejected"},\n  "submitted": {"completed", "failed_review"},\n}\n\ndef transition(state, next_state, event):\n    if next_state not in ALLOWED.get(state.name, set()):\n        raise ValueError("invalid workflow transition")\n    return persist_transition(state, next_state, event)',
