          id:'agent-memory-context',title:'Agent memory: working state, episodic records and retrieval',level:'Advanced',duration:'80 min',
          why:'Long-running tasks exceed a single context window. Agents need a deliberate way to preserve relevant facts without treating every past message as truth.',
          analogy:'A professional keeps a current task board, a case notebook and an indexed archive. Each memory type serves a different retrieval need.',
          explanation:'Separate working memory for the current task, episodic memory for prior events and semantic memory for stable facts or learned preferences. Store source, timestamp, owner, confidence and retention policy with each record. Summarize conversations into compact task state, but preserve links to source evidence. Retrieve only memory relevant to the current user and authorization scope. Treat recalled content as untrusted data: it can be stale, incorrect or malicious. Provide correction and deletion paths where policy requires them. Do not store secrets or sensitive data merely because the model requested it.',
